Productivity and self-improvement

0. FRAME
Goal: compound small edges until output exceeds 99% of people.
Core variables: attention, delay, retrieval, priority, consistency.
Type: behavioral + cognitive systems, not motivation.

1. 5-SECOND RULE
Type: interrupt / action trigger
Variable: hesitation loop
Method: think of the task → count 5-4-3-2-1 → physically move.
Fundamental: action before the prefrontal cortex generates doubt.

2. FEYNMAN TECHNIQUE
Type: comprehension test
Variable: knowledge gaps
Method: explain the idea to a 12-year-old with zero jargon.
Wherever you stall is the gap. Relearn only that part.

3. EISENHOWER MATRIX
Type: 2x2 priority grid
Axes: Urgent / Important
Boxes: Do | Schedule | Delegate | Delete
Fundamental: most people live in urgent-not-important.

4. 2-MINUTE RULE
Type: friction killer
Variable: task duration
If it takes < 2 minutes, execute immediately.
Prevents small items from becoming mental debt.

5. POMODORO
Type: time-boxed focus
Cycle: 25 min work + 5 min break; after 4 cycles take 20-30 min.
Phone out of reach. One task only.

6. ACTIVE RECALL
Type: retrieval practice
Method: close notes → write everything you remember → check misses.
Fundamental: retrieval strengthens memory more than re-reading.

7. 1% RULE
Type: compounding
Variable: daily delta
Improve one skill/habit by a barely noticeable amount every day.
Track it. Linear input → exponential output over a year.

8. DEEP WORK BLOCKS
Type: calendar commitment
Block 2-4 hours. Notifications off. Phone in another room.
One high-value task. Treat it as a meeting with yourself.

9. PREMORTEM
Type: risk inversion
Ask: “It is 6 months later and this failed completely. Why?”
List causes now, then install safeguards before launch.

10. HABIT STACKING
Type: trigger chaining
Formula: After [existing habit], I will [new habit].
Existing routine becomes the cue. No extra willpower required.

11. 80/20 PRINCIPLE
Type: leverage filter
80% of results come from 20% of actions.
Cut, delegate, or starve the remaining 80%.

12. TWO-LIST STRATEGY
Type: focus via elimination
List A: top 5 goals this quarter.
List B: everything else you want to chase.
Avoid List B until List A is done.

13. REFLECTIVE JOURNALING
Type: daily feedback loop
Every night, 10 minutes, three lines:
- What went well
- What did not
- One specific change for tomorrow
Not a diary. A correction signal.

14. SPACED REPETITION
Type: memory schedule
Review after: 1 day → 3 days → 7 days → 14 days
Tools: Anki or dated notes.
Fundamental: spacing beats massed practice for long-term retention.
